<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spring and Autumn period</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 770–481 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spring and Autumn period was an era of Chinese history, roughly the first half of the Eastern Zhou, in which royal power eroded as local lords acted on their own. It began after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanrong">Quanrong</a> invasion destroyed the Western Zhou capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haojing">Haojing</a> in 771 BCE and the court moved east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengzhou">Luoyi</a>. Its name comes from the Spring and Autumn Annals, a chronicle of the state of Lu that tradition links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>. Leading states met in conferences that at times declared one ruler hegemon, the first being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-huan-of-qi">Duke Huan of Qi</a>, while larger states absorbed smaller ones. The gradual Partition of Jin is generally taken to mark its end and the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It fought without the restraints of the <b>Spring and Autumn period</b>, when war had been a gentleman's activity: Qin exploited every weakness, and a nobleman of Wei called it 'avaricious, perverse, eager for profit, and without sincerity'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Chu was the most expansionist state of the <b>Spring and Autumn period</b>, annexing dozens of smaller states, including Shen and Xi in the 680s BCE, Deng, Huang and Jiang, and later Chen in 479 BCE and Cai in 447 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Kong Qiu, as he was named, was born, by the traditional date, in 551 BCE in the small state of Lu, in present-day Shandong, during the <b>Spring and Autumn period</b>, when the authority of the Zhou kings had waned and rival states competed for power.</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Its oldest sections were completed in the early 5th century BCE, during the conflicts of the <b>Spring and Autumn period</b>, to provide supplies and transport routes for the states of Wu and Yue.</blockquote><a href="/work/grand-canal-of-china">Grand Canal of China · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chinese were already familiar with the techniques of wall-building by the <b>Spring and Autumn period</b>, between the 8th and 5th centuries BCE.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall of China · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Art of War is a military treatise of ancient China, dated to the late <b>Spring and Autumn period</b>, roughly the 5th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-art-of-war">The Art of War · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
